{"name":"Tulip Soft Blue","hex":"#c3c4d6","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tulip","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of flowering plant, genus Tulipa.","The flower of this plant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tulip"},{"term":"soft","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Easily giving way under pressure.","Smooth and flexible; not rough, rugged, or harsh."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Be quiet; hold; stop; not so fast."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/soft"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tulip","blocks":[{"text":"Tulips are spring-blooming perennial herbaceous bulbiferous geophytes in the Tulipa genus. Their flowers are usually large, showy, and brightly coloured, generally red, orange, pink, yellow, or white. They often have a different coloured blotch at the base of the tepals, internally. Because of a degree of variability within the populations and a long history of cultivation, classification has been complex and controversial. The tulip is a member of the lily family, Liliaceae, along with 14 other genera, where it is most closely related to Amana, Erythronium, and Gagea in the tribe Lilieae."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/%D7%A6%D7%91%D7%A2%D7%95%D7%A0%D7%99%D7%9D.JPG/330px-%D7%A6%D7%91%D7%A2%D7%95%D7%A0%D7%99%D7%9D.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tulip","wikidata":"Q93201"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Tulipa_alberti_Regel_-_PhytoKeys_%282024%29.jpg/500px-Tulipa_alberti_Regel_-_PhytoKeys_%282024%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Tulipa_alberti_Regel_-_PhytoKeys_%282024%29.jpg/1280px-Tulipa_alberti_Regel_-_PhytoKeys_%282024%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Tulipa_alberti_Regel_-_PhytoKeys_%282024%29.jpg/500px-Tulipa_alberti_Regel_-_PhytoKeys_%282024%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tulipa alberti in Kazakhstan","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATulipa_alberti_Regel_-_PhytoKeys_(2024).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Tulipa_fringed_-_burgundy_lace_-_bulbs.jpg/500px-Tulipa_fringed_-_burgundy_lace_-_bulbs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.or